    {"text":"Jangsa Memorial Honors 772 Forgotten Heroes","decision":"translate"},
    {"text":"While driving along National Route 7 past Yeongdeok, Gyeongbuk—a region renowned for its snow crabs—I suddenly stopped. I had spotted a massive ship anchored at one side of Jangsa Beach. The name “Jangsa Landing Operation Victory Memorial Hall” and the large inscription “Operation No. 174... Forgotten Heroes!” on the ship’s hull caught my eye. “What is the Jangsa Landing Operation?” I asked nearby campers relaxing in the area, but they remained silent. After touring the memorial hall and the memorial park adjacent to the ship (Munsanho), where 772 student volunteers aged 16 to 19 are commemorated with plaques and memorial stones, a sense of guilt overwhelmed me. These individuals were casualties of a feint operation launched along the East Coast in September 1950, during the Korean War (6·25 War), to divert enemy forces while UN troops executed the Incheon Landing Operation. A sigh escaped me: “I’m sorry for not knowing.”","decision":"translate"},
